#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203875 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de17e8f9-e3dc-5ba0-b6c9-327aa8cc10e0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Best One Dimensional Reduction Given SVD of Data"}] :question/id #uuid "d6f2c4b7-eb2d-5d32-bcd8-8ec9443c14da" :question/math-academy-id "e-18786" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4 \\times 2$ matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1.40 & -0.62 \\\\ 0.01 & 0.43 \\\\ -0.85 & 1.19 \\\\ -0.56 & -1.00\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ where:\n\n$U|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.689 & -0.424 & 0.495 & 0.318 \\\\ 0.143 & -0.241 & -0.524 & 0.804 \\\\ 0.695 & -0.188 & 0.650 & 0.244 \\\\ -0.149 & 0.853 & 0.241 & 0.439\\end{bmatrix}; Σ|= \\begin{bmatrix}2.075 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.294 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}; V|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.709 & -0.706 \\\\ 0.706 & -0.709\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance." :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $X^TX$. Using the SVD\n\n$$\nX = U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nand the orthogonality of $U$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX^{T}X &= (UΣV^{T})^{T}(UΣV^{T}) \\\\\n&= VΣ^{T}U^{T}UΣV^{T} \\\\\n&= VΣ^{T}ΣV^{T}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\Sigma^T\\Sigma$ is diagonal with the squared singular values on the diagonal, the eigenvectors of $X^TX$ are the columns of $V$, and the eigenvalues are the squared singular values. The largest eigenvalue corresponds to the first singular value\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = 2.075\n$$\n\nso the first principal component is $v_1$, the first column of $V{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-0.709 \\\\ 0.706\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo obtain the one-dimensional version of the data, we project $X$ onto $v_1$ by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nXv_{1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1.40 & -0.62 \\\\ 0.01 & 0.43 \\\\ -0.85 & 1.19 \\\\ -0.56 & -1.00\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-0.709 \\\\ 0.706\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1.43 \\\\ 0.30 \\\\ 1.44 \\\\ -0.31\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we can compute this projection using $u_1 \\sigma_1$, where $u_1$ is the first column of $U$. Indeed, from the SVD,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nXv_{1} &= UΣV^{T}v_{1} = UΣ\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = U\\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = u_{1}σ_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u_1$ and\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = 2.075\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-0.689 \\\\ 0.143 \\\\ 0.695 \\\\ -0.149\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms the result.\n\n**Watch out!** The vector $v_1$ itself is the first principal component *direction* — a unit vector in feature space. It is not a one-dimensional representation of the data. The one-dimensional representation is $Xv_1$ (or equivalently $u_1\\sigma_1$), which is a $4 \\times 1$ vector containing the projection of each data point onto this direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203876 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"}] :question/id #uuid "a17de4fa-f7c3-5038-b9fa-03780393b360" :question/math-academy-id "e-18840" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\times 3$ matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1.21 & -0.45 & -1.17 \\\\ -0.52 & 0.83 & 1.19 \\\\ -0.18 & -1.37 & 0.41 \\\\ 1.41 & -0.12 & 0.48 \\\\ 0.51 & 1.11 & -0.91\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ where:\n\n$U|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.177 & 0.014 & 0.637 & -0.004 \\\\ 0.275 & -0.054 & -0.847 & 0.36 & 0.274 \\\\ -0.213 & -0.653 & 0.171 & -0.068 & 0.703 \\\\ 0.542 & -0.181 & 0.459 & 0.678 & -0.05 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.711 & 0.206 & -0.022 & 0.655\\end{bmatrix}; Σ|= \\begin{bmatrix}2.272 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.031 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.659 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}; V|= \\begin{bmatrix}0.724 & 0.019 & 0.69 \\\\ 0.422 & 0.779 & -0.464 \\\\ 0.546 & -0.627 & -0.555\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nFill in the blanks below:\n\nWe should pick components based on\n$XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX$\n\nIf we wish to preserve $75\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to\n$XXXXXXXXXXXXXX$." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible.\n\nWe should pick components based on $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}}$.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = 2.272, \\qquad \\sigma_2 = 2.031, \\qquad \\sigma_3 = 1.659\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is\n$\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^3 \\sigma_i^2} = \\dfrac{2.272^2}{2.272^2 + 2.031^2 + 1.659^2} \\approx 42.9\\%$.\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is\n$\\dfrac{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^2 \\sigma_i^2}{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^3 \\sigma_i^2} = \\dfrac{2.272^2 + 2.031^2}{2.272^2 + 2.031^2 + 1.659^2} \\approx 77.1\\%$.\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $42.9\\%$ of the variance, while\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $77.1\\%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/f3/f35a54aa598a0c65fc14ae807b60ddd6dcb68a36dd4f0ba5089fae12ec7a9495.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $75\\%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203877 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f11c96-89e5-5662-81b6-06cff80a4cf2" :knowledge-point/title "Using PCA to Dimensionally Reduce an Uncentered Dataset"}] :question/id #uuid "dcbf83b0-b552-52d6-97d3-9bc3ac6b0823" :question/math-academy-id "e-18844" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\times 3$ matrix\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3.265 & 1.400 & 6.558 \\\\ 5.373 & 0.956 & 1.656 \\\\ 7.349 & 0.797 & -0.459 \\\\ 3.926 & 0.148 & 2.597 \\\\ 2.758 & 0.803 & 3.345\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nLet's walk through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset." :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{the direction in feature space}}$ with the $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{most}}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{orthogonal}}$ to the first $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{principal component}}$ with the most variance.\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{standardize the data}}$.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-0.68 & 1.29 & 1.49 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.30 & -0.42 \\\\ 1.52 & -0.05 & -1.25 \\\\ -0.33 & -1.50 & -0.06 \\\\ -0.96 & -0.04 & 0.24\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{std} &= UΣV^{T}, where \\\\\nU &= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.688 & 0.370 & -0.421 & 0.197 & 0.417 \\\\ 0.160 & 0.258 & 0.226 & 0.910 & -0.168 \\\\ 0.642 & 0.396 & -0.101 & -0.081 & 0.644 \\\\ 0.147 & -0.753 & -0.451 & 0.353 & 0.289 \\\\ -0.260 & -0.271 & 0.747 & 0.038 & 0.547\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\\nΣ &= \\begin{bmatrix}2.825 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.939 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.543 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\\nV &= \\begin{bmatrix}0.607 & 0.503 & -0.615 \\\\ -0.383 & 0.864 & 0.327 \\\\ -0.696 & -0.037 & -0.717\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is\n$\\dfrac{2.825^2}{2.825^2 + 1.939^2 + 0.543^2} \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{66.3\\%}$.\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is\n$\\dfrac{2.825^2 + 1.939^2}{2.825^2 + 1.939^2 + 0.543^2} \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{97.6\\%}$.\n\nThus, we have that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $66.3\\%$ of the variance, while\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $97.6\\%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{2}$ dimensions, since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting $v_1, v_2, v_3$ for the columns of $V$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\nX_{\\text{red}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{X_{\\text{std}} \\cdot [v_1, v_2]}\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V$.\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X$. Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6f25b04-4b91-598e-a565-be3663d6a848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203562} {:answer/id #uuid "c72dffd8-1d94-5753-ab91-0de31a1bf0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203563} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4134ab-9707-5b12-b3b2-b92ef0ef55b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203564} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1c7755-a2c7-544b-8147-4c1e1d7335fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203565}] :answer-field/id #uuid "673f2cfc-466c-5530-bf38-03cc0d0b363a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203561}] :question/id #uuid "d3d0f846-dcec-5a1e-a313-ed34c8131231" :question/math-academy-id "q-355899" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5 \\cdot 4$ matrix\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}-1.11 & -1.51 & -0.99 & -1.19 \\\\ -0.58 & 1.16 & 1.49 & 0.35 \\\\ -0.29 & 0.03 & 0.04 & -0.68 \\\\ 1.43 & 0.57 & -0.84 & 1.42 \\\\ 0.55 & -0.25 & 0.3 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = UΣV^{T}$ where:\n\n$U|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.761 & 0.185 & 0.263 & 0.342 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.309 & -0.738 & 0.28 & 0.285 & 0.447 \\\\ -0.169 & -0.143 & -0.022 & -0.866 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.538 & 0.63 & 0.338 & 0.013 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.084 & 0.067 & -0.859 & 0.225 & 0.447\\end{bmatrix}; Σ|= \\begin{bmatrix}3.125 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.362 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.699 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.455 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}; V|= \\begin{bmatrix}0.49 & 0.509 & -0.625 & -0.333 \\\\ 0.572 & -0.337 & 0.479 & -0.573 \\\\ 0.25 & -0.761 & -0.55 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.608 & 0.22 & 0.278 & 0.71\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on\n{{field-1}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f11c96-89e5-5662-81b6-06cff80a4cf2" :knowledge-point/title "Using PCA to Dimensionally Reduce an Uncentered Dataset"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15eb4a1-c754-53ba-aa20-b209310569fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the direction in feature space" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203567} {:answer/id #uuid "573534b3-1f1c-5d7a-a3db-5c47db32aaed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the datapoint" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203568}] :answer-field/id #uuid "55b225a2-d4bf-5ffc-a3e4-eb6303440b4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203566} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8c884b0-f9c5-5876-a802-4627daf55590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "most" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203570} {:answer/id #uuid "258d6491-b7c0-528f-b3a1-d1559f1edd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "least" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203571}] :answer-field/id #uuid "712f0fe1-5360-5f67-bc0a-139dda8d0dc5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203569}] :question/id #uuid "e5a752c5-890a-5f08-b05f-1f3c318cda03" :question/math-academy-id "q-355955" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\cdot 3$ matrix\n\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}5.659 & 2.361 & 1.245 \\\\ 2.543 & 0.071 & -1.476 \\\\ -1.128 & -4.766 & -4.235 \\\\ 5.697 & -3.658 & -1.704 \\\\ 1.099 & -4.975 & -3.309\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is\n{{field-1}}\nwith the\n{{field-2}}\nvariance."}]}
